Recall that if $\mathcal C$ is a category and $X_\bullet$ is a simplicial set, a \emph{diagram} $X_\bullet\to\mathcal C$ is a map of simplicial sets $X_\bullet\to N_\bullet\mathcal C$ where $N_\bullet\mathcal C$ denotes the \emph{nerve}\footnote{An $n$-simplex $\Delta^n\to N_\bullet\mathcal C$ is a diagram $A_0\xrightarrow{f_{01}}A_1\xrightarrow{f_{12}}\cdots\xrightarrow{f_{n-1,n}}A_n$ in $\mathcal C$.  Strictly speaking, $N_\bullet\mathcal C$ is not a simplicial \emph{set} unless $\mathcal C$ is small.} of $\mathcal C$.  Concretely, a diagram $F:X_\bullet\to\mathcal C$ is the data of:
\begin{rlist}
\item For every vertex $v\in X_0$, an object $A_v\in\mathcal C$.
\item For every edge $e\in X_1$ from $v_0$ to $v_1$, a morphism $f_e:A_{v_0}\to A_{v_1}$ in $\mathcal C$.
\setcounter{rcountsave}{\value{rcounti}}
\end{rlist}
